 """Decide which files in a project tree are excluded by its .packignore files.
 
-This currently delegates to `packignore`, the legacy exclusion engine (see
-docs/PACKIGNORE.md), via the client in tools/.
+This is a pure-Python reimplementation of `packignore`, the legacy exclusion
+engine. docs/PACKIGNORE.md describes it only loosely; the rules below are the
+ones `packignore` actually applies:
+
+* The tree is looked at as on a case- and normalization-insensitive file
+  system: paths whose components are equal after Unicode case folding and
+  canonical normalization are the same entry. The first spelling seen (in
+  walk order) is kept, and for files the last contents win. A path that is
+  both a file and a directory is an error.
+* A file named exactly `.packignore` is read in a directory unless that
+  directory is itself excluded. Its patterns apply to paths below it.
+  Lines are capped (see the limits below), and so is the tree.
+* Lines are split with str.splitlines() and stripped of surrounding
+  whitespace. Empty lines and lines starting with `;` are skipped (`#` is
+  *not* a comment). A leading `!` negates. Trailing `/`s make the pattern
+  match directories only; leading `/`s are dropped. The pattern is anchored to
+  its `.packignore` directory if it had a leading `/` or contains a `/`,
+  otherwise it matches names at any depth. Backslash is an ordinary character.
+* Braces are then expanded: the first `{` and the first `}` after it enclose
+  comma-separated alternatives (not re-expanded); the rest of the pattern is
+  expanded recursively. `[` and `]` are literal.
+* `*` matches any run of characters except `/`, `?` exactly one such
+  character. In an anchored pattern a segment that is exactly `**` matches
+  zero to three path components (so a trailing `/**` also matches the
+  directory itself). Elsewhere `**` is just `*`.
+* A path's status is decided by the patterns matching the path itself; if
+  none does, it inherits its parent directory's status. So a negation can
+  re-include a file inside an excluded directory, but an excluded directory's
+  own `.packignore` is never read.
+* Among the patterns matching a path, one from a deeper `.packignore` wins;
+  within one file the most specific wins (the most characters other than
+  `*?/{},`), and ties go to the later line.
 """
 
